{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q020_syntaxin_fragment_reclassification","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08 (current release, periodic update 2.08-2026-08-16) the fragment structures are NOT treated as a separate or different fold. 1br0 chain A (d1br0a_, sunid 17772), all three chains of 1ez3 (d1ez3a_, d1ez3b_, d1ez3c_; sunids 17768-17770) and 1dn1 chain B (d1dn1b_, sunid 302344) are all classified at a.47.2.1. That is class a (all-alpha), fold a.47 'STAT-like', superfamily a.47.2 't-snare proteins', family a.47.2.1 't-snare proteins'. The 1br0 and 1ez3 domains sit under the protein 'Syntaxin 1A N-terminal domain' (sunid 47663), species Rattus norvegicus (sunid 47664). The 1dn1 B domain sits under the 'automated matches' protein (sunid 191190), species Rattus norvegicus (sunid 311131), and is still in the same family. 1dn1 chain A is a different protein and is classified separately at e.25.1.1.

Biological interpretation: the full-length 1dn1 B domain is the reference for the real syntaxin N-terminal domain. It is an Habc-type antiparallel three-helix bundle. The 1br0 and 1ez3 entries are truncated or partial constructs of that same domain, so their placement is consistent with the full domain. SCOPe has no separate 'fragment fold'. A fragment is placed in the family of the domain it is part of when it carries enough of the structural signal to be recognised. The fold assignment is therefore not changed by fragment status, and the apparent fold change that the question implies should not be read as a real fold difference.

Limits: (1) The assignment is a curated structural and evolutionary judgement. It is not proof that a fragment folds autonomously or has the same stability, conformation or function as the intact domain. A truncated helical bundle may be missing helices or the packing that defines the fold, and its coordinates, such as an NMR ensemble, may differ from the full-length protein. (2) Fold, superfamily and family are different strengths of claim. The fold (a.47) is a structural grouping that includes STAT-like helical bundles, so fold membership alone does not imply homology. The superfamily (a.47.2) is the evolutionary claim. The family (a.47.2.1) means a close relationship. (3) Do not transfer function, such as SNARE complex assembly, Munc18 binding or membrane-fusion roles, from a fragment entry to the whole protein, or the reverse, using classification alone. Check the construct boundaries, the SEQRES/ATOM residue ranges and the experimental context in the PDB. (4) For templates and representatives, prefer the most complete, high-quality full-domain entry (1dn1 B) over fragments. Remember that 1dn1 B is filed under an 'automated matches' protein, not under the manually curated 'Syntaxin 1A N-terminal domain' protein node, so look at the lineage and not just the family label. (5) Domain boundaries and fragment handling can change between SCOPe releases, so cite the release when you report an assignment. I confirmed these placements only for 2.08. I did not retrieve earlier releases, so I cannot say from the API what the original placement of 1br0 and 1ez3 was or when it changed.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/search?q=1br0","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1br0/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ez3/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47664","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47663","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47662","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47661","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47654","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/302344","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/311131","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/191190","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/17768","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1dn1b_"]}]}
